Grove PATCH-29: count lock-tx-B RPC errors CONSECUTIVELY, and never abort a
bid whose remedy is on chain A

Upstream's escalation in checkXmrBidState reads:

    rpc_error_count = self.countBidEvents(bid, EventLogTypes.LOCK_TX_B_RPC_ERROR, cursor)
    if rpc_error_count < 10: ... log it ...
    else: "Too many consecutive RPC errors" -> BidStates.BID_ERROR

countBidEvents counts every LOCK_TX_B_RPC_ERROR row the bid has EVER logged.
Those rows are permanent history; nothing clears them on a successful findTxB.
So the count is a LIFETIME total wearing the word "consecutive", and it is
monotonic: once a bid has logged ten of them, the threshold is met forever and
EVERY subsequent transient blip re-runs the abort.

That is not theoretical. Live mainnet bid 000000006a9c9d96...9bb980,
2026-09-07, taker on an LTC/XMR swap with both legs locked and the
counterparty already gone:

  13:20:56  Temporary RPC error checking lock tx B (9/10): no connection to daemon
  13:21:53  Temporary RPC error checking lock tx B (10/10): no connection to daemon
  13:22:53  Too many consecutive RPC errors (10), aborting swap      -> BID_ERROR
  15:23:47  (state re-derived as 14)
  15:23:50  Too many consecutive RPC errors (10), aborting swap      -> BID_ERROR

The second abort landed THREE SECONDS after the bid came back, on the first
error of a fresh outage, because the counter was already at ten. The bid then
held BID_ERROR for seven hours across 540 consecutive checkBidState polls.

BID_ERROR is terminal for the adaptor-signature state machine: checkXmrBidState
has no branch for it (unlike checkBidState, which carries an explicit
`pass  # Wait for user input`). So the abort did not pause the swap, it ended
the engine's participation in it. What the bid was waiting for at the time was
the coin-A lock-refund SWIPE -- the taker's only remedy once a counterparty
stalls after both locks, and the thing that pays the taker the coin they were
buying. It survived only because the node happened to be restarted, which
re-derived state 14 from chain.

Two changes, both narrow:

  1. COUNT CONSECUTIVELY. A run of failures is tracked in memory on the client
     (`_lock_tx_b_rpc_runs`, bid_id -> int) and CLEARED the moment findTxB
     returns. The event rows are still written, because the operator's history
     is worth keeping; they are simply no longer what the threshold reads. A
     restart clears the runs, which is correct: a fresh process has observed no
     failures yet.

  2. NEVER ABORT FROM XMR_SWAP_SCRIPT_TX_PREREFUND. At that state the remedy is
     entirely on chain A -- publish the lock-refund spend (leader) or the swipe
     (follower) when the CSV matures. Chain B is not consulted by either. So a
     chain-B RPC outage is not a reason to stop driving a chain-A timelock, and
     making it one converts somebody else's node outage into the loss of the
     user's only claim. The run counter still logs, and now says plainly why it
     is not escalating.

     XMR_SWAP_SCRIPT_COIN_LOCKED keeps the abort: there the node IS waiting to
     see its own chain-B lock, so an unreadable chain B is a genuine dead end.

Upstream candidate: yes for (1) -- the message already says "consecutive" and
the code does not implement it, so this is a straight defect fix. (2) is
arguably upstream too but is stated here in our own terms.

--- a/basicswap/basicswap.py
+++ b/basicswap/basicswap.py
@@ -479,6 +479,10 @@
 
         self.debug_ui = self.settings.get("debug_ui", False)
         self._debug_cases = []
+        # PWNDA-PATCH-29: bid_id -> length of the CURRENT run of transient
+        # lock-tx-B RPC failures. In memory on purpose: a restart has observed
+        # no failures yet, and the persisted event rows are history, not state.
+        self._lock_tx_b_rpc_runs: dict = {}
         self._last_checked_actions = 0
         self._last_checked_expired = 0
         self._last_checked_expiring_bids_offers = 0
@@ -9257,14 +9261,34 @@
                 bid_changed: bool = False
                 try:
                     bid_changed = self.findTxB(ci_to, xmr_swap, bid, cursor, was_sent)
+                    # PWNDA-PATCH-29: a success ENDS the run. Without this the
+                    # count below is a lifetime total, and the tenth error a
+                    # bid ever sees becomes a permanent death sentence.
+                    self._lock_tx_b_rpc_runs.pop(bid_id, None)
                 except Exception as e:
                     if ci_to.is_transient_error(e):
-                        rpc_error_count = self.countBidEvents(
-                            bid, EventLogTypes.LOCK_TX_B_RPC_ERROR, cursor
+                        # PWNDA-PATCH-29: consecutive, not lifetime. The event
+                        # rows are still written for the operator's history;
+                        # they are just no longer what the threshold reads.
+                        rpc_error_count = self._lock_tx_b_rpc_runs.get(bid_id, 0) + 1
+                        self._lock_tx_b_rpc_runs[bid_id] = rpc_error_count
+                        # PWNDA-PATCH-29: at PREREFUND the remedy is chain-A
+                        # only (the leader's refund spend, or the follower's
+                        # swipe once the CSV matures). Neither reads chain B,
+                        # so a chain-B outage must not stop the timelock being
+                        # driven: BID_ERROR would end the engine's part in the
+                        # one path that still pays this side.
+                        remedy_is_on_chain_a: bool = (
+                            state == BidStates.XMR_SWAP_SCRIPT_TX_PREREFUND
                         )
-                        if rpc_error_count < 10:
+                        if rpc_error_count < 10 or remedy_is_on_chain_a:
                             self.log.warning(
-                                f"Bid {self.log.id(bid_id)}: Temporary RPC error checking lock tx B ({rpc_error_count + 1}/10): {e}"
+                                f"Bid {self.log.id(bid_id)}: Temporary RPC error checking lock tx B ({rpc_error_count}/10): {e}"
+                                + (
+                                    "; not escalating, this bid's remedy is on chain A"
+                                    if remedy_is_on_chain_a
+                                    else ""
+                                )
                             )
                             self.logBidEvent(
                                 bid.bid_id,
@@ -9280,7 +9304,7 @@
                             self.logBidEvent(
                                 bid.bid_id,
                                 EventLogTypes.ERROR,
-                                f"Persistent RPC error after {rpc_error_count} attempts: {e}",
+                                f"Persistent RPC error after {rpc_error_count} consecutive attempts: {e}",
                                 cursor,
                             )
                             bid_changed = True
